{"doc_id": "doc25626", "receipt": {"answer_in_original_body": true, "failed_attempts": [{"attempt": 1, "generated_text": "Taxonomy, the science of defining and naming groups of living organisms based on shared traits, organizes them into a hierarchy of ranks. These ranks, such as species and genus, form the basis of classification, allowing for the creation of higher groupings like families or kingdoms. Carl Linnaeus, the Swedish botanist, is widely recognized as the father of taxonomy. He developed the foundational system known as Linnaean taxonomy, which provides a structured approach to categorizing organisms, and introduced binomial nomenclature, the method used for naming species.", "reason": "missing_gold_answer", "seed": 2096919744, "similarity": null, "temperature": 0.3}, {"attempt": 2, "generated_text": "Taxonomy, the science of defining and naming groups of biological organisms through shared characteristics, establishes a taxonomic hierarchy using ranks like domain, kingdom, phylum, class, order, family, genus, and species. Carl Linnaeus, a Swedish botanist, is widely recognized for his foundational work in this field, notably developing the Linnaean taxonomy system and introducing binomial nomenclature.", "reason": "missing_gold_answer", "seed": 1450295315, "similarity": null, "temperature": 0.5}], "fingerprint": "668cec4791dacd0fb4eb5503115bb0b2e89ad928962fe94a2f814c381028d9e0", "generation_attempts": 3, "gold_answer": "Swedish botanist Carl Linnaeus", "matched_aliases": ["Swedish botanist Carl Linnaeus"], "query_id": "test716", "question_hash": "73ce381e66134ead29e43335417bcd54689fabfae2ae8891e622a6a041aa0fe8", "repair_from": null, "replaced_row_hash": null, "replaced_text": null, "retry_seed": 0, "row_hash": "a1bba2670d569e0d8a5edcd6b3b63ebe249d38695ca3be32bfc11021d787b649", "scope": "all_answer_documents", "seed": 40226780, "similarity": 0.015197568389057751, "source_content_hash": "1a4ab33c702f4af12548e8ed6aae81d461ef45ae684200a273c01198185feb4a", "